WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
Hey there. I'm Jonathan Levav,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:05.115
Professor of Marketing at the Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:05.115 --> 00:00:07.635
As you may have guessed from the title of this video,

00:00:07.635 --> 00:00:09.600
throughout our marketing mini series,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.740
I'm going to review concepts to help you identify new marketing opportunities.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.250
So what is marketing?

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:20.340
Many people hear the word marketing and think it's a synonym for advertising or sales.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.750
But ads in sales are just one tactical component of the marketing process.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.565
What marketing really is,

00:00:26.565 --> 00:00:30.420
is the process of determining the needs and wants of customers and

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:35.040
delivering the desired benefits more effectively and efficiently than competitors,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:37.625
in order to achieve organizational goals.

00:00:37.625 --> 00:00:39.785
You see marketing in action every day,

00:00:39.785 --> 00:00:41.805
products and services you love,

00:00:41.805 --> 00:00:45.175
ads on the television or the Web, promotions,

00:00:45.175 --> 00:00:47.525
end of aisle displays in grocery stores,

00:00:47.525 --> 00:00:53.315
promo e-mails, mailings, targeted ads, marketing is everywhere.

00:00:53.315 --> 00:00:56.390
You might be staring right at marketing and not even know it.

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:58.205
Like for example this video.

00:00:58.205 --> 00:01:02.440
It's part of a marketing effort for our innovation certificate program here at Stanford.

00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:07.580
Out there in the world, you might see the stickers used to identify Uber or Lyft drivers,

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.440
smell fragrances in stores,

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.075
hear music in malls,

00:01:11.075 --> 00:01:14.110
note the chill vibe of a retail space or coffee shop,

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:17.275
find store brands stock near national brands,

00:01:17.275 --> 00:01:20.405
experience stores that don't even sell you anything.

00:01:20.405 --> 00:01:23.090
These are all actually examples of marketing.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.990
Marketing is at the heart of a new product launch for one simple reason,

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.170
successful products meet consumer needs,

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.375
and they do it better than the competition.

00:01:32.375 --> 00:01:35.450
Marketing is the crucial function in a firm that

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.050
enables us to determine and understand what consumers need.

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:42.715
There is no launching a product without marketing, period.

00:01:42.715 --> 00:01:45.680
So join us in this mini series to learn new tips and

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:48.559
examples of how companies like YouTube and Waze,

00:01:48.559 --> 00:01:52.080
and even individuals like Snoop Dogg use marketing successfully.

